detail.abstract:
Housing capacity is commonly inferred from land availability, construction and the physical dwelling stock. Yet these measures do not necessarily capture the capacity of a housing system to sustain recurrent residential life. This paper develops a functional framework for spatially constrained and territorially open housing markets in which physical stock, Effective Residential Capacity (ERC) and Host-Society Residential Capacity (HSRC) are analytically distinct. HSRC denotes the component of effective residential capacity that supports recurrent residence sufficient to sustain the economic, social and institutional functions of the host society. The framework formalises a Residential-Function Displacement mechanism under which intermittent residential use can reduce HSRC when competing uses draw on a sufficiently common and inelastic housing resource. It then derives a host-society welfare condition in which the benefits of openness and intermittent use are weighed against costs transmitted through reduced HSRC and other territorial externalities. The resulting welfare effect has no universal sign, and no universal second-home threshold follows. Policy intervention is therefore conditional on a Three-Stage Gateway requiring evidence of a material HSRC constraint, wider welfare consequences and a residual problem after less restrictive responses. Functional residential zoning is presented only as one possible residual instrument. The central implication is that a territory may preserve or even expand its housing stock while losing part of the residential capacity required to sustain its everyday economic and social life.&nbsp;
